Got it — so each document carries **either** a `phone` field **or** a `mobile` field (never both), and you want the service to (a) query the right one and (b) tell the UI which kind it is. Here's how to adjust the previous design.

## 1. Mapping

```json
{
  "mappings": {
    "properties": {
      "phone":         { "type": "keyword", "copy_to": "number" },
      "mobile":        { "type": "keyword", "copy_to": "number" },
      "number":        { "type": "keyword" },
      "expression":    { "type": "keyword" },
      "dataSource":    { "type": "keyword" },
      "insertionDate": { "type": "date" }
    }
  }
}
```

- `copy_to: number` gives you one field to query when the caller doesn't know the type. Optional — if you can't reindex, use the `bool/should` variant below instead.
- Type is derived from which field **exists**, so you don't need an extra `numberType` field (though adding one at index time is the cleanest long-term option).

## 2. Enum + DTO

```java
public enum NumberType { PHONE, MOBILE }

public record ExpressionDoc(
        String phone,
        String mobile,
        String expression,
        String dataSource,
        Instant insertionDate) {

    @JsonProperty("numberType")
    public NumberType numberType() {
        return mobile != null ? NumberType.MOBILE : NumberType.PHONE;
    }

    @JsonProperty("number")
    public String number() {
        return mobile != null ? mobile : phone;
    }
}

public record PhoneSummary(
        String number,
        NumberType numberType,          // resolved type
        long totalExpressions,
        ExpressionDoc lastExpression,
        List<DataSourceStats> byDataSource) {}
```

## 3. Query builder — discriminate by type

```java
private Query numberQuery(String number, @Nullable NumberType type) {
    if (type == NumberType.PHONE)  return TermQuery.of(t -> t.field("phone").value(number))._toQuery();
    if (type == NumberType.MOBILE) return TermQuery.of(t -> t.field("mobile").value(number))._toQuery();

    // type unknown -> match either field
    // Option A (with copy_to "number"):
    // return TermQuery.of(t -> t.field("number").value(number))._toQuery();

    // Option B (no reindex needed):
    return BoolQuery.of(b -> b
            .should(s -> s.term(t -> t.field("phone").value(number)))
            .should(s -> s.term(t -> t.field("mobile").value(number)))
            .minimumShouldMatch("1"))._toQuery();
}
```

Routing still works: use the number string as the routing value regardless of which field it lands in.

## 4. Summary — add a per-type breakdown

```java
public PhoneSummary getSummary(String number, @Nullable NumberType type) throws IOException {

    SearchResponse<ExpressionDoc> resp = client.search(s -> s
            .index(INDEX)
            .routing(number)
            .size(0)
            .trackTotalHits(t -> t.enabled(true))
            .query(numberQuery(number, type))
            .aggregations("last", a -> a.topHits(th -> th.size(1)
                    .sort(so -> so.field(f -> f.field("insertionDate").order(SortOrder.Desc)))))
            .aggregations("byType", a -> a.filters(f -> f
                    .keyed(true)
                    .filters(fs -> fs.keyed(Map.of(
                            "PHONE",  ExistsQuery.of(e -> e.field("phone"))._toQuery(),
                            "MOBILE", ExistsQuery.of(e -> e.field("mobile"))._toQuery())))))
            .aggregations("byDataSource", a -> a
                    .terms(t -> t.field("dataSource").size(200))
                    .aggregations("last", sa -> sa.topHits(th -> th.size(1)
                            .sort(so -> so.field(f -> f.field("insertionDate").order(SortOrder.Desc)))))),
            ExpressionDoc.class);

    long total = resp.hits().total() != null ? resp.hits().total().value() : 0;
    ExpressionDoc last = firstTopHit(resp.aggregations().get("last").topHits());

    // resolve type: whichever bucket has docs (only one should)
    Map<String, FiltersBucket> typeBuckets = resp.aggregations().get("byType").filters().buckets().keyed();
    NumberType resolved = typeBuckets.get("MOBILE").docCount() > 0 ? NumberType.MOBILE
                        : typeBuckets.get("PHONE").docCount()  > 0 ? NumberType.PHONE
                        : type; // no hits -> echo back what caller asked

    List<DataSourceStats> perSource = resp.aggregations().get("byDataSource")
            .sterms().buckets().array().stream()
            .map(b -> new DataSourceStats(b.key().stringValue(), b.docCount(),
                    firstTopHit(b.aggregations().get("last").topHits())))
            .toList();

    return new PhoneSummary(number, resolved, total, last, perSource);
}
```

If the same digits could theoretically exist as both a phone and a mobile, both buckets will be non-zero — in that case return both counts to the UI (e.g. `Map<NumberType, Long> countByType`) and let the user pick, or require `type` to be explicit.

